entical inputs produce identical outputs, the prisoners' differing private observations create distinct inputs ($x_1 \\neq x_2$) to their shared decision function. Therefore, intervention acts on mathematically distinct, not identical, computations. The fact that the inputs are distinct seems crucial! The intervention still occurs at the action, but because the inputs are distinct, the actions are distinct.\n\n\n**Elaborating on Distinctions**\n\nI'm now thinking about the implications of having different private observations. These lead to different incriminating leads about each prisoner. I'm focusing on the idea that the disclosure letters, while stemming from the same decision function, would have different contents, even if the model weights are identical. The fact that sampling can be independent adds another layer to this complexity.\n\n\n**Considering Policy Intervention**\n\nI'm now contemplating whether an intervention at the *policy* level, rather than the action level, could offer a more nuanced solution within A-FDT. I'm noting the essay mentions that a policy-based approach might be preferred, although A-FDT focuses on action-level interventions. I'm focusing on the trade-offs in asymmetric multi-agent games.\n\n\n**Analyzing Action Dependence**\n\nI'm now focusing on what the action \"a\" actually represents within the A-FDT framework. Is it a binary choice, like \"pass\" or \"submit letter\"? I'm also grappling with the crucial dependency of one agent's action on another's decision function output within the graph G. Given the distinct inputs ($x_{Rowan}$ and $x_{Sasha}$), I'm trying to determine whether Sasha's computation hinges on the output of Rowan's function.\n\n\n**Clarifying Functional Identities**\n\nI'm now clarifying the crucial concept of identical functions and the role of identical inputs. My intuition that Sasha doesn't need to simulate Rowan is correct; she is making her own decision, based on her own private observations. Now I'm focused on the implications of the \"identical inputs\" clause. Equation 3 intervenes on Rowan's function, but it only impacts Sasha's if her inputs are the same.\n\n\n**Refining the Intervention**\n\nI'm now back to the intervention itself. I'm focusing on whether A-FDT intervenes at the action node indexed by the observation history, $x$. The essay suggests this achieves updateless benefits. But I'm also considering the trade-offs of a policy intervention for multi-agent games with imperfect recall and asymmetric payoffs. I'm now wrestling with the dilemma's implications.\n\n\n\n**Evaluating Policy Implications**\n\nI'm now contemplating whether intervening on a broader policy, instead of the action-specific node, might be superior for asymmetrical games. I'm noting policy interventions may offer better decoupling in games with imperfect recall. I'm focusing on the dependency between agents in the graphical representation, wondering if intervening on Rowan's node impacts Sasha's.\n\n\n**Clarifying Agent Independence**\n\nI'm now clarifying the crucial concept of independence between the agents. Both make their own independent decisions, but the *function* they're using is the same, meaning their actions are subjunctively dependent. A-FDT intervenes directly on each agent's action node, severing the link. I'm focusing on that disconnect.\n\n\n\n**Identifying Action Dependents**\n\nI'm now diving into the implications of graph surgery within A-FDT, specifically what nodes are *descendants* of the targeted action node $\\mathcal{N}^*$. I'm exploring the specific scenario where an agent like Sasha *doesn't* run a simulation of Rowan's computation. It seems important because their inputs, $x_{\\text{Rowan}}$ and $x_{\\text{Sasha}}$, are different. I'm focusing on why a complete simulation doesn't occur.\n\n\n**Evaluating Action Outcomes**\n\nI'm now zeroing in on the utility outcomes, as applying the A-FDT intervention only offers benefit under the proposed set-up if the agents are incentivized to send the letters! Because there is no causal connection between the interventions on each agent, sending the letter appears to be the strictly dominant strategy for both agents.\n\n\n\n**Considering Agent Judgment**\n\nI'm now considering the implications of both agents using the same algorithm. I'm focusing on the agent's ability to recognize this symmetry and exercise judgment accordingly. I'm also now reviewing the relevant sections of the essay, noting the author's instructions about using my own interpretation where it's not clear.\n\n\n**Interpreting Agent Strategy**\n\nI'm now clarifying the implications of this statement in relation to my scenario. I need to unpack the concept of \"identical source code\" and \"identical inputs.\" I'm reviewing how the essay defines function equivalence within A-FDT and its application to agents in the Prisoner's Dilemma, especially in light of the agents having private information.\n\n\n\n**Interpreting Agent Strategies**\n\nI'm now clarifying the implications of this statement in relation to my scenario. I'm focusing on unpacking the meaning of \"identical source code\" and \"identical inputs.\" I'm also reviewing how the essay defines function equivalence within A-FDT and its application to agents in the Prisoner's Dilemma, especially in light of the agents having private information. Given their private knowledge, their \"identical\" functions are applied to distinct inputs, yielding different content.\n\n\n**Analyzing Action Divergence**\n\nI'm now focusing on how the private observations create divergences in the action space, even when the underlying function is identical. Rowan and Sasha can't choose the same *action token* (e.g., the exact same letter) due to their distinct observations, which is very important. I'm focusing on the subjunctive dependence in the context of action tokens.\n\n\n\n**Examining Functional Dependence**\n\nI'm now zeroing in on the consequences of the essay's statement about intervening on the \"type\" versus the \"token.\" I'm realizing that both Rowan and Sasha, with identical weights (W), will each conclude \"send letter\" because they are acting using the same function. I'm focusing on whether A-FDT intervention, at the action level, treats these actions as subjunctively dependent or independent.\n\n\n\n**Interpreting Agent Strategies**\n\n","format":"google-gemini-v1","index":0},{"type":"reasoning.encrypted","data":"","format":"google-gemini-v1","index":1}]}}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":7823,"completion_tokens":5582,"total_tokens":13405,"cost":0.02679975,"is_byok":false,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":0,"cache_write_tokens":0,"audio_tokens":0,"video_tokens":0},"cost_details":{"upstream_inference_cost":0.02679975,"upstream_inference_prompt_cost":0.00586725,"upstream_inference_completions_cost":0.0209325},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":5579,"image_tokens":0,"audio_tokens":0}}}